\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-c8, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, N: e4-c3, N: e4-c5, N: e4-d2, N: e4-d6, N: e4-f2, N: e4-f6, N: e4-g3, N: e4-g5, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, K: e8-d7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: g4-f3, B: g4-e2, B: g4-h3, B: g4-f5, B: g4-e6, B: g4-d7, B: g4-c8, B: g4-h5, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is in a semi-open position with several tactical possibilities. White has advanced a pawn to d5 (PWF1 on d5), which is currently unsupported and could become a target. My pieces are relatively well-placed: the knight on e4 is centrally active, the dark-squared bishop on g4 exerts pressure, and the queen on d8 has room to operate. However, my kingside is slightly weakened, and castling hasn't occurred yet for either side. The white pawn on d5 is isolated and can be attacked by my pawn on c7 or knight on e4.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: c7-c5 — Strong candidate. Strikes at the center, challenges the d5 pawn immediately, and opens lines for development. This move increases central control and may win material if White cannot defend d5 adequately.\n- N: e4-c5 — Also strong; attacks the d5 pawn and repositions the knight actively. But it allows White to potentially trade knights or push pawns.\n- Q: d8-d6 — Puts direct pressure on d5 and supports a potential capture on d5. However, it risks blocking the diagonal for the bishop or exposing the queen early.\n- P: e7-e5 — Closes the center prematurely and blocks the bishop on f8; less desirable.\n- B: g4-e6 — Could increase pressure on d5 via a battery with the queen, but moves the bishop away from its current pin idea and may allow White to disrupt with f2-f3.\n\nReason: The most principled and dynamic move is P: c7-c5. It strikes at the heart of White’s center, directly threatening to capture on d5. If White recaptures (dxc5), then Nxe4 or Qxd1+ Rxd1 followed by Nxc5 regains the pawn with active play. Even if White defends, Black gains space and initiative. This move fits the strategy of challenging isolated central pawns and gaining central dominance.\n\nAnswer: P: c7-c5"}
