acked? Our pawn on f7, knight on e2? No immediate attacker.\n- To d4? d4 — empty.\n- To c4? c4 — empty.\n- To c6? c6 — black knight.\n- To d6? d6 — empty.\n- To e6? Occupied by our bishop.\n\nBut after Be6+, king cannot stay.\n\nNow, crucially — **after Be6+, can white capture the bishop?**\n\nWhat defends e6? Only the bishop itself — no other black piece.\n\nCan white play d5xe6? That would be king capturing the bishop.\n\nYes — king can capture on e6.\n\nSo after Be6+, white can play Kd5xe6 — capturing the bishop.\n\nThen we lose the bishop for nothing?\n\nBut wait — after Kd5xe6, the king is on e6 — very exposed.\n\nThen we can play **pawn f7-f5**, threatening to open the f-file?\n\nOr better: **pawn f7-f6+**, delivering check?\n\nf7-f6: pawn to f6 — attacks e5 and g5 — not e6.\n\nTo attack e6, need pawn on f7 to go to f5 — then next move f5-f4, but not immediate.\n\nKnight on e2? e2 is white knight — NWF1 — so not ours.\n\nOur knight is on c6 and e2? Wait — e2: NBF1 — that’s a **black knight on e2**!\n\nYes — row 2: e2: NBF1 — black knight on e2.\n\nAnd valid moves include: N: e2-c1, e2-c3, e2-d4, e2-f4, e2-g1, e2-g3 — all knight moves.\n\nIf white king goes to e6 after Kd5xe6, then e6 is attacked by whom?\n\n- Knight on e2: e2 to e6? Not a knight move.\n- Knight on c6: c6 to e5, d4, etc. — not e6.\n- Pawn on f7: f7 to e6? Only diagonally — f7-e6 is diagonal? f7-e6 is one back, one left — yes, diagonal — so **if the pawn is on f7**, it can capture on e6.\n\nBut only if it moves diagonally forward — but e6 is **behind** f7? No: f7 is rank 7, e6 is rank 6 — so e6 is **one rank down** — so from black’s perspective, it\'s **forward**.\n\nBlack pawns move **up** the board — from rank 7 to 8 is forward.\n\nSo from f7, black pawn moves to f6, f5, etc. — downward.\n\nSo f7 to f6 is forward.\n\nTo capture, it goes to e6 or g6 — diagonally forward.\n\nYes — so **f7 pawn can capture on e6**.\n\nSo if white plays Kd5xe6, then we can reply **P: f7-e6**, recapturing — but tha