\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|    |    |    |NWF1|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, R: d8-c8, R: d8-b8, R: d8-a8, R: d8-d7, R: d8-d6, R: d8-d5, R: d8-d4, N: e2-c1, N: e2-c3, N: e2-d4, N: e2-f4, N: e2-g1, N: e2-g3, P: e5-d4, P: e5-e4, K: e8-d7, K: e8-e7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, B: g4-f3, B: g4-h3, B: g4-f5, B: g4-e6, B: g4-d7, B: g4-c8, B: g4-h5, N: g6-e7, N: g6-f4, N: g6-h4, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: Black is in a slightly passive position with White having advanced pawns and active pieces, particularly the knight on e2 and bishop on g4 putting pressure. The black king is still on e8, uncastled, but has not moved (KBF0), meaning castling kingside is still an option if safe. However, the rook on h8 is already disconnected from the center. White’s pawn on e5 is isolated and potentially vulnerable, especially as it can be captured by the black pawn on d7 via en passant (but currently no black pawn adjacent to e5 except after movement). Black has a strong dark-squared bishop on f8 and a knight on g6 eyeing central outposts. The white queen on f3 (implied from QWF1 at f3) could be a target.\n\nKey observations:\n- Black\'s light-squared bishop (c8) is missing — likely captured.\n- Black has a pawn on e5 (PBT1), which just moved one square last turn (not two), so no en passant eligibility.\n- White has a pawn on d4 that might become a threat.\n- Black has a chance to develop actively and challenge the center or exploit weaknesses.\n\nEvaluation of promising moves:\n1. **P: b7-b5** – Advances the queenside pawn, possibly preparing to open lines for the rook on a8 or support a future …b6 and development. But it weakens c5 and may not address immediate central dynamics.\n2. **P: f7-f6** – Supports …e5 push or defends g6 knight indirectly; prepares kingside solidity. However, it weakens the kingside dark squares and blocks the bishop on g4 from retreating to f5 or e6.\n3. **N: g6-e7** – Retreats the knight to reposition via f5 or d6, possibly supporting an attack or central control. It’s flexible but somewhat passive.\n4. **B: f8-c5** – Develops the bishop to a strong diagonal, targeting f2 (weak point near white king) and increasing pressure on the center. This is a very active developing move.\n5. **R: d8-d5** – Places rook on an open file attacking the e5 pawn. Strong idea — immediately challenging White’s center.\n6. **P: e5-d4** – Captures the d4 pawn (if present)? Wait — checking board: there is no d4 pawn. White has pawns on d2? No — looking back: White has pawns on b2, c2, d4? Wait — current state shows PWF1 at d4? Yes! At d4: PWF1 (White pawn on d4). So e5-d4 would capture it! But wait — e5 is occupied by PBT1 (Black pawn), and d4 is diagonally forward-left from e5? No — d4 is left-forward only if on e5 and moving to d4 — yes, that\'s a diagonal capture. So **P: e5-d4** is a valid capture, removing White’s d4 pawn and opening the center.\n\nWait — correction: e5 to d4 is *not* a legal pawn move. From e5, a black pawn moves *forward*, which is toward lower ranks — so down the board. Since Black is on top, forward is toward rank 4, then 3, etc. So from e5, forward is e4, e3, etc. Diagonal captures are to d4 or f4. And d4 has a white pawn (PWF1). So **P: e5-d4** is a capture — yes, and it\'s in the valid move list. So this is a real tactical opportunity.\n\nSo **P: e5-d4** captures a white pawn on d4, opening the center and activating play.\n\nBut what about **B: f8-c5**? That targets