WF1) and d1-rook possibly developed.\n\nWhite has a pawn on d4? No — looking again:\n\n- d4 is empty.\n- e4: empty\n- d1: not shown; actually, KWF1 is on d1? Wait — KWF1 is listed at d1? No — the board shows:\n  - On d4: KWF1 → White King on d4? That can\'t be right.\n\nWait — this seems like a misrepresentation. Let\'s re-express the board correctly from the grid:\n\nRow 8: a8: empty, b8: empty, c8: RBF1 (Black Rook), d8: empty, e8: KBF0 (Black King), f8: BBF0 (Black Bishop), g8: empty, h8: RBF0 (Black Rook)\n\nRow 7: a7: PBF0, b7: empty, c7: empty, d7: empty, e7: empty, f7: PBF0, g7: PBF0, h7: PBF0\n\nRow 6: a6: empty, b6: empty, c6: empty, d6: empty, e6: empty, f6: empty, g6: empty, h6: BWF1 (White Bishop)\n\nRow 5: a5: PWF1 (White Pawn), b5–h5: empty\n\nRow 4: a4: RWF1 (White Rook), b4: empty, c4: NBF1 (Black Knight!), d4: KWF1 (White King?!), e4: empty, f4: empty, g4: empty, h4: PWF1 (White Pawn)\n\nRow 3: a3: empty, b3: PWF1 (White Pawn), c3: empty, d3: empty, e3: empty, f3: BBF1 (Black Bishop), g3: empty, h3: empty\n\nRow 2: a2: empty, b2: empty, c2: PWF0 (White Pawn, unmoved), d2: empty, e2: NWF1 (White Knight), f2: empty, g2: BWF1 (White Bishop), h2: empty\n\nRow 1: a1: empty, b1: empty, c1: empty, d1: empty, e1: empty, f1: empty, g1: empty, h1: RWF1 (White Rook)\n\nWait — this is chaotic. The White King is on d4? That’s extremely exposed! KWF1 at d4 — yes, according to the board. That means the White King has moved and is currently on d4, likely in great danger.\n\nThat changes everything.\n\nMy Black Knight is on c4 (NBF1), just one square away from the enemy king on d4. The knight attacks d4, but cannot capture because the king is there — but more importantly, the king is wide open and likely in check or about to be attacked.\n\nBut is the knight attacking d4? From c4, knight moves: a3, a5, b2, b6, d2, d6, e3, e5 — none of which is d4. A knight on c4 does NOT attack d4. It attacks:\n\n- c4 → b2, a3, a5, b6, d6, e5, e3, d2 — yes, those are the valid moves