lack is up in development.\n\nAnother candidate: **N: f2-e4** — centralizes knight.\n\nBut **R: c8-c5** wins a pawn cleanly.\n\nAre there faster tactics?\n\nWhat about **B: g7-c3**? Bg7-d4-e5-f6-g7 — no, g7 to c3: g7-f6-e5-d4-c3 — that’s five left, five down — diagonal.\n\nYes — B: g7-c3 is listed.\n\nc3 has a white pawn (PWF1).\n\nSo **B: g7-c3** attacks the pawn on c3.\n\nIs c3 defended?\n\nWhite pawn on c3 — probably c2-c3.\n\nDefended by whom? Possibly the king on d2 — d2 to c3 is adjacent — yes! d2 and c3 are neighboring squares.\n\nSo c3 is defended by the king.\n\nSo Bxc3 would be captured back by Kxc3.\n\nNot winning material.\n\nWhereas c6 is **not** defended.\n\nSo better to go after c6.\n\nR: c8-c5 is excellent.\n\nBut is there a better move?\n\nWhat about **R: c8-c6**? Directly attacking c6.\n\nIs that possible? Yes, if no piece in between.\n\nc7 and c6: c7 is empty (row 7: only a7, h7, g7 have pieces), c6 has PWF1.\n\nSo R: c8-c6 is possible — directly attacking and capturing the pawn.\n\nWhy is **R: c8-c6** in the valid moves list?\n\nBecause it’s a capture: Rxc6.\n\nEven better than c5 — why play c5 when you can take immediately?\n\nBecause: is c6 protected?\n\nWe said no — but could it be en prise?\n\nYes — and no reason not to capture.\n\nBut wait: after Rxc6, White plays QxR? But White has no queen.\n\nWhat can White do? Only king moves or other pieces.\n\nNo piece defends c6.\n\nSo Rxc6 wins a pawn cleanly.\n\nMoreover, it opens the c-file for the other rook.\n\nSo **R: c8-c6** is superior to R: c8-c5.\n\nBut is it safe?\n\nDoes White have a counter?\n\nFor example, after Rc8-c6, can White play Nf2 something? But f2 has a black knight.\n\nWhite knight is on g1.\n\nAfter Rc6, White might play Nxh7+? Ng1xh7+?\n\nh7 has PBF0 — black pawn.\n\nNxh7+ checks the black king.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nNg1 to h7: g1→h7 — file +1, rank +6 — not a knight move.\n\nKnight moves: from g1, possible: h3, f3, e2.\n\nNot h7.\n\nSo no knight fork.\n\nWhite has no immediate tactic.\n\nTherefore, **R: c8-c6** wins a pawn safely.\n\nOther moves like N: f2-e4 are good but slower.\n\nCapturing the pawn is immediate material gain.\n\nTherefore, best move is **R: c8-c6**.\n\nBut wait — is that in the valid move list?\n\nYes: “R: c8-c6” is listed.\n\nPerfect.\n\nReason: It wins a free pawn on c6, which is undefended, and improves rook activity with no apparent counterplay from White.\n\nAnswer: R: c8-c6'}
