\n1 |    |RWF1|KWF1|    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: f5-f4, R: f6-e6, R: f6-d6, R: f6-c6, R: f6-b6, R: f6-a6, R: f6-f7, R: f6-f8, R: f6-g6, R: f6-h6, B: g2-f1, B: g2-h1, B: g2-f3, B: g2-e4, B: g2-d5, B: g2-c6, B: g2-b7, B: g2-a8, B: g2-h3, B: g7-h6, B: g7-f8, B: g7-h8, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on g8 (KBF1), currently not in check. My position has several active possibilities: a rook on f6 (RBF1) that is centrally placed and can exert pressure, two bishops (on g7 and g2), and pawns on h7 and f5. White has a somewhat disorganized structure, with loose pieces — notably a white bishop on g2 that is undefended and potentially vulnerable. The black rook on f6 is attacking the center and can increase pressure along the 6th rank or file. Additionally, the pawn on f5 is one step from supporting an advance to f4, which opens lines.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: f5-f4 – Advances the pawn, supports potential central control, but doesn’t immediately threaten anything significant.\n- R: f6-f7 – Attacks the undefended white bishop on g2 (since after Rf7, it attacks h7 and indirectly g2 via discovered attack if needed). However, Rf7 also threatens mate on f1 if not stopped.\n- R: f6-h6 – Targets h2, possibly preparing coordination with the h-pawn or increasing mating threats on the back rank.\n- B: g7-h6 – Develops more activity, eyes the weakened light squares around White’s king (e.g., g1), but less urgent.\n- B: g2-d5 – A strong diagonal move for the dark-squared bishop, targeting key central squares and potentially threatening the white king long-term.\n- K: g8-f7 – King moving into the open; risky without necessity and loses castling rights (though already lost).\n- P: h7-h5 – Pushes the h-pawn, prepares potential ...h4, possibly provoking weaknesses or enabling rook lifts.\n\nAmong these, R: f6-f7 stands out: it attacks the undefended white rook on h1 (via f-file) and also threatens devastating discovered checks or even mate ideas (e.g., if the rook takes on f1, it\'s likely mate or massive material gain). Let’s analyze: After Rf6-f7, the rook lands on f7, attacking the entire f-file behind it. The white king is on g1 (KWF1), rook on h1 (RWF1), and no piece defends f1. So Rxf7 would be followed by Rxh1+, winning at least the rook.\n\nBut wait — is Rf6-f7 safe? White cannot recapture immediately because no piece guards f7. And after Rf7, the rook is supported by the king on g8. Even if White plays Rh1xh7, then Rh7xg7+ wins the bishop and keeps pressure.\n\nAlternatively, Rf6-f8+ would be check, but f8 is occupied by empty square — but in the valid moves list, R: f6-f8 is allowed, meaning it\'s legal. But f8 is not attacked by anything, so f7 gives more flexibility.\n\nBut crucially: R: f6-f7 is not just positional — it wins material. Because after Rf7, White cannot prevent the capture of either the h1 rook or must sacrifice material to stop it.\n\nWait — correction: Rf6-f7 does **not** directly attack h1 — it only controls the f-file. To get to h1, it needs to go Rf7-f1 or Rf7-h7. But still, placing a rook on f7 increases pressure dramatically. More importantly, **Rf6-f8+** is a check! Because the black rook on f6 moves to f8, delivering check to the white king on g1 (aligned along the 8th rank? No — f8 is same rank as king? King is on g1 — not aligned).\n\nWait — f8 and g1 are not aligned. So Rf6-f8 is not check. It just occupies the 8th rank.\n\nBut now consider: what happens after Rf6-f7? Black plays Rxf7, and then next move can play Rxh7, capturing the pawn on h7, which is defended only by the white pawn on g2. But Rxh7 captures the pawn, and if Bg2xh7, then Bg7xh7 — bishop recaptures, but we’ve won a pawn.\n\nBut better: after Rf6-f7, if White ignores it, Black can go Rf7-h7, attacking the undefended bishop on g2. That bishop has no escape — if Rh7-g7, then Bg7-g6 or something, but actually Rh7-g7 wins the bishop outright.\n\nYes: Rf6-f7 → Rf7-h7 → Rh7xg7, winning the bishop.\n\nEven better: after Rf6-f7, if White plays Bg2-h3, then Rh7-g7 still wins.\n\nSo Rf6-f7 enables a sequence to win the bishop on g2.\n\nAlternatively, Rf6-h6 also prepares Rh6-g6 or Rh6-g7, but f7 is a stronger intermediate square.\n\nAlso, note that Rf6-f7 is protected by the black king on g8, so it’s safe.\n\nAnother strong option: Bg7-h6. This develops the bishop to a strong diagonal, threatening Bg2. But White can play Bh3 or Pd4 or something to block.\n\nBut Rf6-f7 leads to immediate material gain.\n\nNow, looking back at the valid moves list: R: f6-f7 is present.\n\nIs there any risk? If Black plays Rf6-f7, can White play Qd1-h5+? No queen on board. White has no queen. Only pieces are visible.\n\nWhite has rooks, knights, bishops, pawns — no queen mentioned. So no back-rank mate danger.\n\nThus, Rf6-f7 is safe and strong.\n\nCompare to P: f5-f4 — small improvement, not impactful.\n\nB: g2-d5 — good idea, but why let White develop? Also, Bg2 is our own bishop? Wait — no!\n\nCritical mistake: B: g2 — that’s a **white** bishop! In the board:\n\nRow 2: g2 has BBF1 — Black Bishop? Wait no:\n\nWait — row 2: "BBF1" at g2? Let\'s recheck:\n\nFrom board:\n2 |    |    |    |    |    |    |BBF1|    | 2\n\nBBF1 — that means **Black Bishop**, never moved? But earlier I thought it was white.\n\nWait — no: encoding is [Type][Color][2Step][Moved]\n\nSo BBF1 = Bishop - Black - Not 2-step - Never moved.\n\nAh! So g2 has a **black** bishop? But how?\n\nLet’s reconstruct:\n\n- Row 7: PBF0 (a7), BBF1 (g7), PBF0 (h7) — so black bishop on g7\n- Row 2: BBF1 on g2 — another black bishop?\n\nThat’s possible — two bishops.\n\nAnd row 3: NWF1 on h3 — white knight.\n\nSo yes, black has a bishop on g2 — that makes sense: it must have come from somewhere, maybe developed early.\n\nSo B: g2-d5 is a valid move for the **black** bishop on g2.\n\nSimilarly, B: g7-h6 is for the other bishop.\n\nSo both bishops are black.\n\nTherefore, the bishop on g2 (BBF1) can move to d5, which is a powerful central outpost.\n\nBut still, the rook lift Rf6-f7 seems most forcing.\n\nAfter Rf6-f7, Black threatens Rh7, winning the bishop on g2 — because Rh7 attacks g7, where the bishop is.\n\nWait — no: Rh7 attacks g7? Rh7 is on h7, so it attacks g7 only if adjacent — yes, h7 to g7 is one square left — yes, adjacent.\n\nSo if rook goes to h7, it attacks g7 — where the black bishop is? No: g7 has a black bishop — BBF1.\n\nWait — confusion again.\n\nBoard:\n- g7: BBF1 — black bishop\n- g2: BBF1 — another black bishop? That can\'t be — same piece type, but two on board? Yes, bishops can be duplicated after promotion.\n\nBut here, both are marked BBF1 — never moved.\n\nBut a bishop on g2 and g7 — both black — possible.\n\nBut if rook goes to h7, it doesn\'t attack its own bishop.\n\nThe target is **white** pieces.\n\nWhite bishop — where?\n\nOn g2: BBF1 — black color — so it\'s ours.\n\nWhite pieces are marked W.\n\nSo where are white bishops?\n\nNone visible except — row 2: g2 is BBF1 — black.\n\nRow 3: b3: PWF1 — white pawn.\n\nNo white bishop shown.\n\nWhite has:\n- a1: RWF1 — rook\n- b1: KWF1 — king\n- c1: RWF1 — rook? Wait no — row 1: b1 is RWF1? No:\n\nRow 1: a1: empty? No — " |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | ..." — so a1: empty, b1: RWF1 (rook), c1: KWF1 (king), etc.\n\nWait — that can\'t be — king on c1? Unusual.\n\nList:\n1 |    |RWF1|KWF1|    |    |    |    |    | 1\n\nSo white king on c1, rook on b1.\n\nAnd rook on a3? No — a3: RWF1 — rook on a3.\n\nWait — row 3: a3: RWF1 — white rook.\n\nAnd f6: RBF1 — black rook.\n\nSo white rooks: a3 and b1.\n\nBlack rooks: f6 and h8? h8 is empty.\n\nOnly f6 listed.\n\nBack to bishop: is there a white bishop?\n\ng2: BBF1 — black bishop.\n\nSo white has no bishop on g2 — instead, black has a bishop on g2.\n\nHow did it get there? Maybe promoted or developed.\n\nBut regardless, it\'s ours.\n\nSo the piece on g2 is a black bishop — so we control it.\n\nThen, what is the target?\n\nWhite\'s weakness: king on c1, exposed.\n\nBlack has rook on f6, which can go to f2, attacking the back rank.\n\nBut f2 is empty.\n\nRf6-f2 would be powerful — but not in valid moves.\n\nValid rook moves: only f6-e6, d6, c6, b6, a6, f7, f8, g6, h6.\n\nNo f2 — because it\'s not on the same file or rank with clear path? From f6 to f2: blocked by pawn on f4? f4: PWF1 — white pawn on f4.\n\nYes — f4 has PWF1 — white pawn.\n\nSo file f: f6 (black rook), f5 (PBF1 — black pawn), f4 (PWF1 — white), so rook on f6 cannot go below f5.\n\nHence, only up to f8 or sideways.\n\nSo Rf6-f7 or f8.\n\nNow, Rf6-f7: moves to f7, which is currently empty.\n\nf7: in row 7 — files: a7: PBF0, g7: BBF1, h7: PBF0 — f7 is empty.\n\nSo Rf6-f7 is legal.\n\nAfter Rf6-f7, rook is on f7, one step from f8.\n\nBut more importantly, it increases pressure on the 7th rank.\n\nNow, black already has a bishop on g7 — so doubling on the 7th rank could be powerful.\n\nAlso, the rook on f7 supports a future ...Rf8, or ...Re7, etc.\n\nBut is there a tactic?\n\nConsider: Black plays Rf6-f7.\n\nWhite has no immediate counter.\n\nThen Black can follow with Bg7-h6, attacking the knight on h3? h3 has NWF1 — white knight.\n\nYes! h3 has a white knight (NWF1).\n\nSo Bg7-h6 attacks Nh3.\n\nAnd if White doesn’t protect it, Black wins the knight.\n\nMoreover, if White plays g2-g3, then Bh6-g5, pinning or increasing pressure.\n\nBut even better: after Rf6-f7, if White plays Nxh6, then Rxh6, but we don’t want that.\n\nNo: we play Bg7-h6 to attack Nh3.\n\nBut Nh3 is on h3, Bh6 is on h6 — same file? h6 to h3 — three down — yes, vertical? No — bishop moves diagonally.\n\nBg7 to h6 — that’s diagonal: g7 to h6 is one square northeast — yes.\n\nFrom h6, can it attack h3? No — h6 to h3 is straight down — only rook/queen.\n\nBishop on h6 attacks along diagonals: a7, b8, or g5, f4, e3, d2, c1 — ah! c1 has the white king!\n\nYes! Diagonal h6-c1 — passes through g5, f4, e3, d2, c1.\n\nSo Bg7-h6 creates a **discovered attack** possibility.\n\nIf the rook moves off the f-file, we can have discovered check.\n\nBut more importantly, Bh6 directly attacks the white king on c1 — **it’s a check!**\n\nOh! Critical insight.\n\nAfter Bg7-h6, the bishop checks the king on c1.\n\nAnd the king has limited escape: c1 to b1, d1, b2, d2, c2.\n\nb1 has a white rook — so only d1, c2, d2, b2.\n\nBut is the check covered?\n\nWho blocks? Only if a piece can interpose.\n\nBut the diagonal h6-c1: squares g5, f4, e3, d2.\n\nf4 has a white pawn (PWF1), d2 is empty.\n\nSo White can block with pawn or piece.\n\nBut importantly, if no block, king must move.\n\nBut the key point: **Bg7-h6 is a check**, and it’s very strong.\n\nNow, can we prepare it?\n\nCurrently, the black rook is on f6, blocking the diagonal? No — the bishop on g7 is on g7, and h6 is free.\n\nSo Bg7-h6 is a legal move — and it’s in the valid moves list: B: g7-h6.\n\nAnd it delivers check.\n\nAfter Bg7-h6+, White must respond.\n\nPossible responses:\n- Block with f4 pawn: f4-f5? But f4 is white — PWF1 — but f5 has a black pawn (PBF1) — so f4 cannot go to f5 — blocked.\n- Block with d2 — but no piece on d2.\n- Block with e3 — no piece.\n- Move king: c1-b1, c1-d1, c1-c2, c1-d2.\n\nb1 has a rook — so Kb1 is possible.\n\nBut then Black can play Rxh6, winning the bishop? No — we moved the bishop.\n\nAfter Bh6+, if Kd1, then Black can play Rf6-f1+! — attacking the rook on b1 and giving check.\n\nBecause from f1, rook checks along first rank.\n\nWhite king on d1, rook on f1 gives check.\n\nThen Kc2, and Rxb1 — winning the rook.\n\nOr if king goes to c2, then Rf1 attacks b1 anyway.\n\nBut more simply: after Bh6+, suppose Kd1, then Rf6-f1+ Ke2 (only), then Rf1xb1 — win a rook.\n\nAnd we’re up material.\n\nEven better: if White doesn’t have time to save the rook.\n\nBut is there a refutation?\n\nWhat if White plays g2-g3? But g2 has a black bishop — BBF1 — so White can’t play g2-g3 — that’s our piece.\n\ng2 is occupied by a black bishop — so White has no pawn on g2.\n\nWhite pawns: a3: none, b3: PWF1, f4: PWF1, h4: PWF1.\n\nSo no g-pawn visible.\n\nSo White cannot play g2-g3.\n\nTherefore, after Bg7-h6+, the only replies are:\n- King moves: Kb1, Kd1, Kc2, Kd2\n- Or interpose — but no piece can reach f4, e3, d2 in one move.\n\nFor example, can a white piece go to f4? Already occupied by white pawn.\n\ne3: no piece can go there — knight on h3 can go to f2 or g1, not e3.\n\nSo no interposition possible.\n\nTherefore, **Bg7-h6+ is check with no block — only king move.**\n\nAfter any king move, Black can play Rf6-f1+, forking or checking.\n\nExample:\n- Bh6+, Kd1\n- Rf6-f1+!\n- Now Ke2 or Kc2 or Kd2\n- Then Rf1xb1 — wins the rook.\n\nMaterial gain: we give up a bishop but win a rook — excellent trade.\n\nEven if White saves the rook, we’re up in development.\n\nBut actually, we don’t lose the bishop — White isn’t forced to take it.\n\nAfter Bh6+, White moves king, then we play Rf6-f1+, and White must deal with check.\n\nThe rook on b1 is hanging.\n\nSo best line:\n1. ... Bg7-h6+ \n   - Kh1? No — king on c1. Kb1, Kd1, etc.\n   - Say Kd1\n2. ... Rf6-f1+ \n3. Ke2 (only, since Kc2 still attacked by rook? f1 attacks c1, b1, a1, d1, e1, etc. — yes, Kc2 is on c2, not on f-file — so not attacked. So Kc2 is possible.)\n   - But Rf1 attacks all of rank 1 and file f.\n   - So after Rf6-f1+, the king cannot go to c1, b1, a1, d1, e1, f1 — all attacked.\n   - So only escapes: c2, d2, e2.\n\nSay Ke2.\n\nThen Black plays Rxh1 — wait, h1 is empty.\n\nOr simply Rxg2? g2 has our own bishop.\n\nNo — we can play ...Rxb1 — but b1 has white rook.\n\nRf1xb1 — yes, captures the rook.\n\nSo after Rf6-f1+, whatever king move, Black plays Rf1xb1, winning the rook.\n\nWe lose the bishop on h6? Only if White captures it — but after Rf1xb1, White is down a rook.\n\nNet: we traded a bishop for a rook — +2 pawns in value.\n\nAnd we have active pieces.\n\nSo Bg7-h6 is extremely strong.\n\nNow, is it in the valid moves? Yes: B: g7-h6\n\nBut wait — after Bh6, is the bishop protected? No — White could play Nxh6, but only after king move.\n\nBut after Bh6+, White must address the check first — cannot capture.\n\nSo the sequence is forced.\n\nTherefore, Bg7-h6 is winning.\n\nCompare to Rf6-f7 — solid, but not as forcing.\n\nP: f5-f4 — slow.\n\nSo best move is B: g7-h6\n\nAnswer: B: g7-h6'}
