s on f6, so moving the pawn from f5 to f4 just vacates f5.\n\nBut what is currently on f5? PBF1 — black pawn.\n\nAnd on f6: RBF1 — black rook.\n\nSo they are stacked vertically: rook on f6, pawn on f5 — same file. So pawn is behind rook? That’s fine.\n\nBut if Black plays **P: f5-f4**, it moves the pawn forward one square — now on f4.\n\nThat doesn’t hinder the rook — rook stays on f6.\n\nBut why do that? It advances a pawn toward the center, possibly freeing f5 for other use, but not immediately powerful.\n\nAlternatively, **R: f6-f7** attacks the White pawn on g7? There is no g7 pawn — g7 is empty. h7 has a black pawn.\n\nf7 is occupied? Row 7: a: PBF0, h: PBF0 — so f7 is empty. So Rf6-f7 is possible.\n\nRf6-f7: places rook on seventh rank — **very strong**, especially since White’s king is on c1, likely with pawns still on second rank. A rook on the 7th rank can attack pawns and restrict the king.\n\nCurrently, White has pawns on a2? Not shown — only known pawns: PWF1 on a6 (wait — a6? That’s deep in Black’s side), and PWF1 on b3, and PWF1 on g5.\n\nWait — let\'s map all pieces:\n\nBlack:\n- King: g8\n- Bishop: f1\n- Rook: f6\n- Pawns: f5, h7, and a7 (a7: PBF0)\n\nWhite:\n- King: c1\n- Rooks: a3 (RWF1), b2 (RWF1)\n- Pawns: a6 (PWF1), b3 (PWF1), g5 (PWF1)\n- Bishop: f1? No — f1 is BBF1 → Black bishop\n\nWait — f1: BBF1 → Black bishop on f1 — that’s deep in White’s side!\n\nSo Black has **two pieces inside White’s camp**: bishop on f1 and rook on f6, plus a pawn on f5 and another on a6? a6: PWF1 → White pawn.\n\nLet me clarify ownership:\n\nFrom board:\n\n- a7: PBF0 → Black pawn\n- a6: PWF1 → White pawn\n- b3: PWF1 → White pawn\n- b2: RWF1 → White rook\n- a3: RWF1 → White rook\n- c1: KWF1 → White king\n- f1: BBF1 → Black bishop\n- f5: PBF1 → Black pawn\n- f6: RBF1 → Black rook\n- g5: PWF1 → White pawn\n- g8: KBF1 → Black king\n- h7: PBF0 → Black pawn\n\nSo Black has:\n- Active pieces deep in White’s position: **Rook on f6**, **Bishop on f1**\n- Pawns on f5, h7, a7\