4 doesn’t cover f6. So safe capture.\n\nAlso consider: **B: a8-d5** — develops bishop to strong diagonal, eyes c4, e3, etc. But less urgent than winning a pawn.\n\nAnother idea: **Q: d8-d6** — supports potential ...Bd5 or prepares ...Rd8, but again, less direct than capturing.\n\n**Q: d8-f6** stands out as a free pawn grab with strong follow-up.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended pawn on f6 increases Black's material advantage and improves piece activity. The queen on f6 exerts strong pressure on the center and White’s vulnerable king position. It also may open lines for further attacks, especially since White’s king on c3 is already exposed. This move maximizes winning chances by combining material gain with initiative.\n\nAnswer: Q: d8-f6"}
