l\n- c2-b3: attacks b3 pawn? No — b3 has a white pawn (PWF1), so c2-b3 would be capturing that pawn.\n- c2-d3: threatens d3, but d3 is empty\n- c2-e4: continues along diagonal\n- c2-f5: directly captures the white rook on f5! That’s huge.\n\nc2-f5: This move captures the white rook on f5, which is undefended. White has no piece defending f5. After BBF1 takes RWF1 on f5, I win a full rook for free — massive material gain.\n\nOther king moves like g8-h7 or g8-h8 are slow and do nothing compared to such a tactical win.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended rook on f5 with the bishop is a winning move, as it gains significant material (rook for free) and cripples White’s position. There is no apparent defense or recapture. This maximizes my chances of winning immediately.\n\nAnswer: B: c2-f5"}
