 strong — it forks or pins pressure on the c3 king, as moving to d3 puts a direct diagonal attack on the White king (if protected, still dominates).\n- However, the White king on c3 is adjacent to d3 — so B: f5-d3 gives check! This is critical.\n\nYes — B: f5-d3 delivers check to the White king on c3. This is a forcing move, highly advantageous in an endgame.\n\nAdditionally, after Bf5-d3+, White must respond (e.g., move king to b3, b4, d2, or capture if possible — but they can't capture the bishop unless a piece is there, which it isn’t). This gains tempo and improves my position significantly.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the bishop maximizes pressure, forces White into a defensive response, and improves my king’s chances to advance later. No other move offers such a direct threat.\n\nAnswer: B: f5-d3"}
