k to the white king on d2. Since the king is on d2 and e2 is diagonally accessible from d3 (bishop’s diagonal), this is both legal and highly disruptive. It forces the white king to move (likely to c1, c3, or e3), potentially losing time or walking into further threats. It also activates the bishop to a very aggressive square.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the bishop increases pressure on White’s poorly positioned king, disrupts their coordination, and gains tempo. Among all options, **B: d3-e2** is the most forcing and strategically dominant move.\n\nAnswer: B: d3-e2'}
