\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: a6-b5, B: a6-c4, B: a6-d3, B: a6-e2, B: a6-f1, B: a6-b7, B: a6-c8, K: f7-e6, K: f7-e7, K: f7-e8, K: f7-f6, K: f7-f8, K: f7-g6, K: f7-g7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my position is quite precarious. My king on f7 is exposed and surrounded by limited friendly pieces. The only active piece I have is the bishop on a6 (BBF1) and the king itself. White has a pawn on b4 (PWF1), which just moved to an advanced position, and another on h7 (PWF1), dangerously close to promotion. My king is currently on f7, with escape squares toward e8, e7, e6, g7, g6, f6, and f8. However, moving to some of these may lead to being trapped or losing material. The bishop on a6 has potential to exert long-range pressure, especially along the a6-f1 diagonal, potentially targeting key central or weak squares near White’s king.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Bishop moves: \n  - B: a6-b5 — attacks c4 but doesn't gain much; can be chased by pawn.\n  - B: a6-c4 — hits center, pressures d3/e2, possibly eyeing kingside.\n  - B: a6-d3 — targets e2 square, possibly threatening discovered ideas if king moves.\n  - B: a6-e2 — very aggressive! This attacks the white king directly on d1 (though not visible here), but in this case, it threatens check on e2, which might be decisive if the white king is on d1 or c1. But wait — White’s king is actually on d2 (KWF1 at d2). So Be2+ would be check!\n  - B: a6-f1 — reaches corner, but less active unless there's a target.\n  - B: a6-b7 — captures pawn? Wait — b7 is empty. Invalid idea.\n  - B: a6-c8 — passive, retreats backward.\n\nWait — checking board again: a7 is empty, b7 is empty, so no capture. All bishop moves are non-capturing.\n\nBut crucially: **B: a6-e2** gives check to the white king on d2! Because bishop from a6 to e2 is diagonal (a6→b5→c4→d3→e2), and d2 is one square away diagonally from e2? No — e2 to d2 is adjacent horizontally, not diagonal. So is e2 attacking d2?\n\nNo — bishop on e2 attacks along diagonals: so it hits f3, g4, h5, and also d1, c0… but d2 is not on that diagonal. d2 is one square west of e2 — horizontal, not diagonal. So **Be2 does NOT give check**.\n\nSo none of the bishop moves deliver immediate check.\n\nNow evaluating king moves:\n- K: f7-e6 — moves into center, somewhat risky.\n- K: f7-e7 — lateral, neutral.\n- K: f7-e8 — retreats to back rank.\n- K: f7-f6 — advances, but could be trapped later.\n- K: f7-f8 — up the file.\n- K: f7-g6 — heads toward kingside.\n- K: f7-g7 — natural retreat, connects with potential shelter.\n\nHowever, notice: White has a pawn on h7 (PWF1), which is just one square from queening. If White plays h7-h8=Q next move, they will promote and likely win. That pawn is on h7, and it's white, so it's on rank 7 — meaning it's already on its 7th rank, so it can promote on h8 next move.\n\nBut whose turn is it? It's Black's turn now. So if I don’t stop it, White will play h7-h8=Q next move and promote.\n\nTherefore, **I must prevent h7-h8=Q**.\n\nHow?\nOnly way is to **capture the pawn on h7**, but my king is on f7 — can it reach h7?\n\nKing from f7 to g7 to h7 — yes! One move: **K: f7-g7** doesn’t capture, but **K: f7-h7** isn’t even a valid move listed.\n\nWait — valid king moves are:  \n[K: f7-e6, K: f7-e7, K: f7-e8, K: f7-f6, K: f7-f8, K: f7-g6, K: f7-g7]\n\nNo **K: f7-h7** — because that’s two squares over — illegal.\n\nSo king cannot capture h7 pawn in one move.\n\nIs there any other piece that can capture on h7? Only my bishop on a6 — but a6 to h7 is not diagonal. a6 is light square (a dark-square bishop), h7 is dark square — so no.\n\nSo **no black piece can capture the pawn on h7**.\n\nBut wait — what square is the white pawn on? The board says:\n\nRow 7: |PBF0|    |    |    |    |KBF1|    |PWF1| 7\n\nSo files: a7: PBF0 (Black Pawn), b7: empty, ..., f7: KBF1 (Black King), g7: empty, h7: PWF1 (White Pawn)\n\nSo yes — White has a pawn on h7, ready to promote.\n\nAnd it's Black's turn.\n\nIf I do nothing, White will play h7-h8=Q and promote to queen, winning easily.\n\nSo I must **prevent promotion** — but I cannot capture it.\n\nAlternative: **deliver checkmate or force a draw?**\n\nBut unlikely.\n\nOr perhaps: **promote my own pawn?** Do I have a pawn?\n\nYes — on a7: PBF0 — Black pawn on a7, hasn't moved.\n\nFrom a7, it can go to a6 (already did?), no — current position shows PBF0 on a7? Wait no:\n\nBoard row 7: a7: PBF0 — yes, Black pawn on a7.\n\nBut valid moves don't include any pawn moves — only bishop and king moves.\n\nWhy? Because pawns on a7 can move to a6 or a5? But a6 is occupied?\n\nWait — a6 is where the bishop is: BBF1 on a6.\n\nSo no — the pawn is on a7, bishop is on a6? That can't be — same square?\n\nWait — look again:\n\nRow 7: a7: PBF0 — Black pawn\nRow 6: a6: BBF1 — Black bishop\n\nSo pawn on a7, bishop on a6 — different squares.\n\nBut then why no pawn moves in valid move list?\n\nThe valid moves provided are only:\n[B: a6-b5, ... , K: f7-g7]\n\nNo pawn moves listed.\n\nThat means either:\n- The pawn on a7 is blocked\n- Or the system filtered out invalid moves\n\nBut a7 pawn (PBF0) — hasn't moved — so it can go a7-a6 or a7-a5.\n\nBut a6 is occupied by my own bishop (BBF1) — so cannot move.\n\nHence, pawn is **blocked** by own bishop.\n\nSo no pawn moves possible.\n\nThus, I have only king and bishop moves.\n\nBack to threat: White will promote next move unless I can do something.\n\nBut I cannot stop h7-h8=Q.\n\nUnless... does any of my moves **deliver check**, forcing White to respond, thereby delaying the promotion?\n\nLet’s see:\n\nCan **B: a6-e2** attack anything important?\n\ne2 — is there a piece? Board shows e2 is empty.\n\nWhite king is on d2 (KWF1).\n\nFrom a6, bishop moves:\n- a6 → e2: diagonal? a6 (dark), b5 (light), c4 (dark), d3 (light), e2 (dark) — wait, a6 is file a, rank 6 — a is dark, 6 even → dark square. e2: e is dark, 2 even → dark square. So yes, same color.\n\nPath: a6-b5-c4-d3-e2 — all dark squares? b5: b light, 5 odd → dark? Wait, chessboard: a1 is dark (assuming standard coloring).\n\nStandard: a1 = dark (black), so a-file: a1(dark), a2(light), a3(dark), etc.\n\nSo a6: a (file 1), 6 → (1+6)=7 → odd → dark square.\n\nb5: b(2)+5=7 → odd → dark.\n\nc4: 3+4=7 → dark.\n\nd3: 4+3=7 → dark.\n\ne2: 5+2=7 → dark.\n\nSo yes, all same color — so bishop can go a6-e2.\n\nNow, does it attack the white king on d2?\n\ne2 to d2: e2 (5,2), d2 (4,2) — adjacent horizontally. Not diagonal. So no.\n\nClosest diagonal attack would be to c1 or f3, etc.\n\nSo no check.\n\nSimilarly, no other bishop move gives check.\n\nKing moves: none of them attack anything.\n\nSo **none of my moves give check**.\n\nTherefore, White is free to promote next move.\n\nAfter I move, White plays h7-h8=Q, promotes to queen.\n\nThen I’m down a queen with no compensation — lost.\n\nBut wait — is the pawn on h7 really able to move?\n\nIt’s PWF1 — White pawn on h7, already moved (so came from h6), and not en passant eligible.\n\nIt can move h7-h8 and promote.\n\nYes.\n\nSo unless I can **checkmate White this move**, or **force a draw**, I lose.\n\nIs there any way?\n\nCheckmating White: White king on d2.\n\nWhat can attack d2?\n\nMy king on f7 — too far.\n\nMy bishop on a6 — can it get to c4? a6-c4 is a move.\n\nc4 to d2: c4(3,4), d2(4,2) — difference (1,-2) — knight move, not diagonal.\n\nSo no.\n\nd3? a6-d3 — d3(4,3), d2(4,2) — vertical, not diagonal.\n\ne2 — as before, not attacking d2.\n\nSo no check possible.\n\nStalemate? Am I stalemated now? No — I have many legal moves.\n\nAfter I move, game continues.\n\nSo all paths lead to White promoting and winning.\n\nBut maybe I should **maximize resistance** or look for swindle.\n\nAlternatively — is the pawn on h7 actually **blocked**?\n\nBy what? h8 is empty — nothing blocking.\n\nUnless my king or bishop can control h8?\n\nCan my bishop go to f1? a6-f1: a6 to f1 — a6,b5,c4,d3,e2,f1 — yes, diagonal.\n\nf1 is empty.\n\nBut f1 doesn't help.\n\nOr g6? Not reachable.\n\nWait — what if I play **K: f7-g7**?\n\nThen king moves to g7.\n\nNext move, White promotes h7-h8=Q.\n\nBut then I have king on g7 — adjacent to h8.\n\nSo after Qh8+, I can capture the queen: Kxh8.\n\nWould that work?\n\nYes!\n\nIf I move king to g7 now, then after White plays h7-h8=Q, giving check, I play Kxh8, capturing the queen.\n\nThen White has no immediate second queen, and I survive.\n\nOtherwise, if I don’t go to g7, and say I move bishop, then after h8=Q+, my king is on f7, and if it goes to f6 or e7, I still lose the queen without compensation.\n\nBut by going to g7, I **prepare to capture the promoted queen**.\n\nThat’s the best practical chance.\n\nEven though material is still bad, at least I don’t lose instantly.\n\nMoreover, after Kxh8, White may not have enough material to mate — let's see: White has king on d2, and possibly the b4 pawn.\n\nBut king + pawn vs lone king — can win if pawn promotes, but I can try to block.\n\nBut at least it prolongs the game.\n\nAlternatively, if I leave king on f7, after h8=Q+, I must move — say to f6, f8, g7, g6 — but then White has a queen, and I have no counterplay.\n\nBut by going to g7 first, I enable the capture.\n\nSo **K: f7-g7** is the best move — it prepares NxQ on h8.\n\nNo other move allows that.\n\nEven better: after Kf7-g7, if White still plays h7-h8=Q, then Kxh8, and White has only king and one pawn left — I might hold a draw.\n\nWhereas other moves lead to immediate loss.\n\nTherefore, despite the bleak situation, **K: f7-g7** is the optimal move.\n\nReason: It positions the king to capture the enemy queen after promotion on h8, preventing immediate overwhelming disadvantage and offering drawing chances.\n\nAnswer: K: f7-g7"}
