\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: a6-b5, B: a6-c4, B: a6-d3, B: a6-e2, B: a6-f1, B: a6-b7, B: a6-c8, K: g7-f6, K: g7-f7, K: g7-f8, K: g7-g6, K: g7-h6, K: g7-h7, K: g7-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and currently have only my king on g7 and a bishop on a6. The white king is on c3, which is quite centralized, but far from my pawns. White has two pawns: one on b4 (PWF1) and one on h7 (PWF1), both already moved and not en passant eligible. My king is on g7, with limited mobility, and my bishop is on a6, aiming diagonally toward the center and potentially threatening key squares. White’s king is somewhat exposed, but not immediately vulnerable. My goal should be to activate my pieces, prevent White's pawns from advancing, and aim for a favorable endgame.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Bishop moves:\n  - B: a6-b5 – Attacks the pawn on b4, which is undefended. Strong tactical move.\n  - B: a6-c4 – Centralizes the bishop, eyes the kingside, but doesn't pressure b4 directly.\n  - B: a6-d3 – Reaches deep into White’s side, threatens control over key central/diagonal squares, but may be less urgent.\n  - B: a6-e2/f1 – Too far and slow; likely losing tempo.\n  - B: a6-b7/c8 – Retreats or sideways; passive, no immediate benefit.\n\n- King moves:\n  - K: g7-f6/h6 – Approaching the center or supporting potential action, but f6 might be useful to stop pawn advances or support bishop.\n  - Other king moves (f7, f8, g6, h7, h8) are mostly lateral or backward – less active.\n\nBest candidate moves:\n1. B: a6-b5 – Immediately captures the undefended b4 pawn if allowed, gaining material. But wait: is b4 occupied? Looking at board: b4 has PWF1 → yes, there is a white pawn on b4. So a6-b5 would capture it.\n   However, after Bxb5, White can play Kb3 or Kd3, attacking the bishop. But since the bishop is on b5 and not defended, White can recapture if king comes close.\n\nWait — is the pawn on b4 really capturable?\n\nLooking again: current board shows:\n- b4: PWF1 (White pawn)\n- a6: BBF1 (Black Bishop)\nValid move list includes B: a6-b5 — so the system allows it. That means either the pawn is unprotected, or the capture is legal.\n\nBut after Bxb5, White king on c3 can play Kc4 or Kb3, attacking the bishop. Since we’d be trading bishop for pawn, and White gets a more active king, this may not be favorable unless necessary.\n\nAlternatively, B: a6-c4 prevents the white pawn on b4 from advancing (pins it indirectly?) — actually, no pin, but controls c4 and prepares to pressure d3/e2 later.\n\nBut even better: B: a6-b5 wins a pawn for free **only if** White cannot immediately recapture. After Bxb5, White must respond. Their king can go to b3 or c4, but neither attacks b5 immediately. Wait:\n\n- From c3, king can go to b3 (a2, b4, c4 are other options). b3 is adjacent to c4 and b4, but **not** to b5. b5 is not under attack from c3 or any other white piece.\n\nSo after Bxb5, White cannot recapture immediately. The bishop is safe.\n\nTherefore, B: a6-b5 wins a clean pawn.\n\nMoreover, White’s remaining pawn on h7 is isolated and can be mopped up later.\n\nAlso, our king can march toward h7 to stop its promotion.\n\nThus, capturing the pawn is strong.\n\nOther good option: K: g7-f6 — activates the king, centralizes, prepares to march to g5 or f5 to stop h-pawn.\n\nBut winning a pawn with tempo is better.\n\nReason: Capturing the b4 pawn with the bishop gains material immediately and improves activity. The bishop is safe after the capture, as the white king cannot reach it in one move. This puts Black a pawn up in an otherwise symmetrical position, significantly increasing winning chances.\n\nAnswer: B: a6-b5"}
