n on a5 to advance freely. However, since it's on a5 and my king is on f5, it will take several moves for me to reach it unless I start advancing immediately. The optimal plan is to bring the king toward c7 or b7 to stop the pawn’s promotion. To do that, the shortest path is via e4-d5-c6-b7.\n\nAmong the options, K: f5-e4 is the most efficient way to begin centralizing the king with intent to march it to the queenside. It gains a tempo and improves king activity significantly.\n\nReason: Centralizing the king is paramount in endgames, especially when opposing a lone pawn. K: f5-e4 is the first step in cutting off White’s pawn expansion and preparing to capture it. Passive bishop moves delay progress and risk letting White push their pawn with support from their king.\n\nAnswer: K: f5-e4"}
