ving it further may not be bad, but without targets, it’s aimless. The king is not in check, but could become vulnerable if the bishop doesn’t create counterplay.\n\nKey insight: The white pawn on a6 is one move away from queening (a6-a7-a8). If I don't act, White can play a7, then promote next move. I must prevent that. The only piece that can influence a8 is my bishop. From a4, the bishop controls a8 via the a4-e8 diagonal. That means **B: c2-a4** puts the bishop on a square where it can control the promotion square.\n\nTherefore, **B: c2-a4** is critical — it prevents White from safely promoting by supporting a future a7 push unless White uses their king.\n\nReason: Preventing the enemy pawn from promoting is the highest priority. The bishop move to a4 directly influences the a-file and the promotion square a8, restricting White’s winning plans. It also activates the bishop to maximum effect in an otherwise dire material situation.\n\nAnswer: B: c2-a4"}
