\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: c2-b1, B: c2-d1, B: c2-b3, B: c2-a4, B: c2-d3, K: e4-d3, K: e4-d4, K: e4-d5, K: e4-e3, K: e4-e5, K: e4-f3, K: e4-f4, K: e4-f5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on e4, and White has a pawn on a6 (PWF1) and a king on a2 (KWF1), along with a bishop on c2 (BBF1). The only active piece I have is my king and this bishop. The position is highly unusual, likely a late endgame scenario. White’s pawn on a6 is advanced but not immediately threatening promotion since it's blocked by nothing and my king is far away. However, I must act precisely due to limited material.\n\nEvaluation:  \n- Moving the bishop from c2 offers several options: capturing on b1 or d1 (both empty in reality, so just positional), or developing to safer/central squares like b3, a4, d3.  \n- King moves from e4 can centralize (e5, f5, etc.) or approach the queenside (d3, d4, d5) toward White’s pawn.  \n- The most urgent issue is preventing White’s pawn on a6 from advancing and promoting. However, my king is too far (e4 → a6 is 5 moves), and the bishop cannot stop it alone (wrong color diagonal? Need to check: a6 is black square, bishop on c2 is on light square — BBF1: starts on c2 which is dark square? Wait: c2 is a dark square (c=file3=odd, 2=even → dark), a6 is also dark → same color! So bishop *can* control a6).\n\nWait: c2 is dark square, a6 is dark square → yes, my bishop *can* influence a6. But currently it's on c2, and a6 is on the same diagonal (a6-c4-e2… no — wait: a6 to c2 is a6-b5-c4-d3-e2-f1 → yes, that's a diagonal. So c2 is on the a4–f8 diagonal? Let's clarify:\n\n- Diagonals: c2 connects to b1, d3, e4, f5, g6, h7 and to b3, a4. So a4 is reachable, but a6 is *not* on that diagonal. a6 is on the a6-b5-c4-d3-e2-f1 diagonal → so c2 is NOT on that. c2 is on c2-b1 and c2-d3-e4-f5-g6-h7 and c2-b3-a4. So a4 is reachable, but a6 is on d3-e2-f1 diagonal? No: a6-b5-c4-d3-e2-f1 — yes, so d3 and e2 are on that diagonal. So if I play Bc2-d3, then the bishop will be on d3, which is on the a6-f1 diagonal. From d3, it controls c4, b5, a6 — perfect! So Bd3 threatens to capture on a6 if the pawn advances.\n\nAlso, White’s king is on a2 — very passive. If I can control a6 and prevent the pawn from advancing safely, I can draw or even win if they blunder.\n\nBut I must also consider king activity. My king on e4 can march toward a6 via d5-c6-b6-a6, but that takes time.\n\nBest plan: Use bishop to control the a6-pawn’s promotion path, while advancing my king.\n\nPromising moves:\n- B: c2-d3 — excellent; gets on the a6-f1 diagonal, threatening to capture the pawn if it advances. Also supports future king invasion.\n- B: c2-a4 — also hits the diagonal toward a6, but a4 is less central and doesn’t directly guard b5 or c4 as well. Still good, but d3 is better.\n- K: e4-d5 — heads toward the queenside, but slow. Without bishop support, may not catch pawn.\n- Other bishop moves like b1, d1, b3 are useless — off the key diagonals.\n\nReason: B: c2-d3 is best because it places the bishop on the critical diagonal leading to a6, prepares to capture the pawn if it advances, and coordinates with the king’s approach. It maximizes control and minimizes White’s winning chances.\n\nAnswer: B: c2-d3"}
