 square away from a2 (White’s king), but more importantly, **b1** lies on the same diagonal as a8? No — b1 is light-square, a8 is dark-square. Bishop on d3 is on light squares (d3 is light), so it cannot control a8 (which is dark). Therefore, **my bishop cannot ever reach a8 or attack the pawn on a7**.\n- My king is on e4; the distance to a7 is large. Can my king reach a7 in time? Let's calculate:\n  - Current position: Ke4\n  - Needs to go to a7 to capture the pawn before it promotes.\n  - Distance: from e4 to a7 is 4 files left, 3 ranks up → at least 4 moves needed (e.g., e4→d5→c6→b7→a7).\n  - But White needs only **one move**: a7→a8=Q.\n  - So I cannot stop the pawn by capturing it with my king.\n\nThus, **I cannot prevent the pawn from promoting** unless White makes a mistake.\n\nBut wait — is the pawn actually able to move?\n\nLet’s look again: the pawn is on a7, color is **PWF1** = White Pawn, already moved (so not on starting rank 2), and currently on rank 7. Its last move was from a6 to a7 (hence F1 = not two-step, so no en passant). It is now on a7, so its next move can be a7→a8 with promotion.\n\nSo yes, White can and will promote next move unless I make a threat so severe that White must respond.\n\nBut it's **Black's turn now**, so I can make one move before White promotes.\n\nIf I do **not** deliver check or create an immediate threat, White will simply play a7→a8=Q and win.\n\nTherefore, my best chance is to **deliver check** or create a **mating net** or **threaten immediate material gain** such that White must respond instead of promoting.\n\nLet’s examine my valid moves:\n\nValid moves:\n- B: d3-c2\n- B: d3-b1 ← this checks the White king!\n- B: d3-e2\n- B: d3-f1\n- B: d3-c4\n- B: d3-b5\n- B: d3-a6\n- K: e4-d4\n- K: e4-d5\n- K: e4-e3\n- K: e4-e5\n- K: e4-f3\n- K: e4-f4\n- K: e4-f5\n\nNow, **B: d3-b1** delivers check: bishop moves from d3 to b1, attacking a2 — which is where the White king is!\n\nAfter B: d3-b1+, the White king must get out of check.\n\nPossible escapes for White king:\n- a2→b1? But b1 is occupied by the bishop after the move — no.\n- a2→b2? Is b2 attacked? After bishop is on b1, it controls the diagonal a2-b1-c0? No — b1 is occupied by black bishop, so the diagonal stops there. The bishop on b1 attacks a2 and c2. So a2 is under attack — king cannot stay.\n\nSo king must go to b2 (if not attacked).\n\nIs b2 attacked?\n\nCurrent bishop on b1 attacks along the diagonal: a2, b1 — that’s it. It does not attack b2. The square b2 is one right and one up from a2 — not on the same diagonal.\n\nSo White king can escape to b2.\n\nSo after:\n1. ... B: d3-b1+\n2. Kb2\n\nThen White can still play a7→a8=Q on the next move, and win.\n\nBut now, after promotion, Black might be able to do something?\n\nWait — but after 1... Bb1+, 2.Kb2, then White plays a8=Q, and now White has a queen and king vs Black’s king and bishop.\n\nWhite can easily checkmate.\n\nBut is there any other benefit?\n\nAlternatively, suppose I don’t give check, but threaten something else?\n\nNo — giving check is the only way to delay the inevitable.\n\nBut let’s see: after B: d3-b1+, White is forced to move the king, and **cannot promote immediately** — because it's not checkmate, and king can move to b2.\n\nSo I gain one tempo.\n\nBut still lose.\n\nBut maybe there’s a stalemate idea?\n\nSuppose I make a move that doesn’t check, but leads to stalemate if White promotes?\n\nFor example, if after my move, White has no legal moves except promoting, but promoting leads to a position where their king is stalemated?\n\nUnlikely.\n\nAlternatively: is the White king currently in stalemate if I do nothing?\n\nNo — White has two pieces: pawn on a7 and king on a2.\n\nKing can move to b1 or b2 (both unoccupied), and pawn can move to a8.\n\nSo multiple legal moves.\n\nBut if I play B: d3-c2, then c2 is now occupied.\n\nDoes that block anything?\n\nAfter B: d3-c2, the bishop is on c2, which attacks b1 and d1 (diagonally).\n\nSo b1 is attacked.\n\nNow, White’s king on a2 has:\n- b1: attacked by bishop → illegal\n- b2: still available?\n\nc2 is light square, b2 is dark — so not attacked by bishop.\n\nSo king can go to b2.\n\nPawn can still go to a8.\n\nSo White can still play either Kb2 or a8=Q.\n\nSo no restriction.\n\nBut if I play **B: d3-b1**, I attack a2 directly — check.\n\nOnly then is White forced to deal with it.\n\nOther bishop moves don’t create immediate threats.\n\nNow consider king moves: moving my king further away won’t help.\n\nBut what if I walk into a stalemate trap?\n\nSuppose I do nothing useful — White promotes to queen, then has many ways to avoid stalemate.\n\nSo no.\n\nBack to B: d3-b1 — it’s the only move that gives a check.\n\nEven though White escapes, at least I had a fighting chance.\n\nBut is there a better move?\n\nWait — what about **B: d3-a6**?\n\nThat goes to a6 — which is on the same file as a7.\n\nBut doesn't attack a7 — bishop on a6 attacks b5, c4, etc., not vertically.\n\nNo.\n\nOr B: d3-f1 — far away.\n\nNo.\n\nSo only **B: d3-b1** delivers check.\n\nAnd since it’s the only forcing move, it maximizes practical chances — perhaps White blunders and walks into a corner.\n\nMoreover, after B: d3-b1+, if White mistakenly plays Ka1??, then I can play Ba2#? Wait:\n\n- After ... Ka1, bishop on b1 attacks a2 and c2, but not a1 directly.\n- From b1, bishop cannot attack a1 — different color? a1 is dark, b1 is light — yes, bishop is on light-squared diagonals, a1 is dark — so no.\n\nSo no mate.\n\nBut if White plays Kb1??, then I capture: Bxb1+ and win the king? No, can't capture king.\n\nBut opponent would never play Kb1 when in check — illegal.\n\nSo only legal is Kb2.\n\nSo after B: d3-b1+, Kb2, then a7-a8=Q, and game over.\n\nStill, among all moves, **this is the only one that delays the loss**.\n\nAll others allow White to promote immediately without penalty.\n\nTherefore, **B: d3-b1** is the best move — it’s the only one that gives a check and forces White to respond.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the bishop to b1 is the only forcing move available, preventing White from immediately promoting the pawn to a queen. While White can escape with Kb2, this gains one tempo and maximizes practical winning/drawing chances, possibly capitalizing on an opponent error.\n\nAnswer: B: d3-b1"}
