\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: d3-c2, B: d3-b1, B: d3-e2, B: d3-f1, B: d3-c4, B: d3-b5, B: d3-a6, K: e4-d4, K: e4-d5, K: e4-e3, K: e4-e5, K: e4-f3, K: e4-f4, K: e4-f5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on e4, and my bishop is on d3. The only white piece visible is a pawn on a7 (PWF1), which is likely a promoted or advanced pawn. White’s king is on a2. The position is highly unusual, with both kings exposed and far from their starting positions. There are no immediate threats of checkmate, but king safety and activity are critical.\n\nMy bishop on d3 has several diagonal options, mostly toward the queenside and center. My king on e4 is in the center and can move to various squares, including advancing toward the center or retreating. The absence of most pieces suggests this is an endgame scenario where king and minor piece coordination is key.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Bishop moves:\n  - d3-c2: Retreats toward the center-left; supports potential king activity but loses central control.\n  - d3-b1: Moves to corner, very passive, poor square for a bishop.\n  - d3-e2: Supports f1 and g3, covers key central dark squares, and connects with king.\n  - d3-f1: Very passive, retreats to corner, little utility.\n  - d3-c4: Central outpost, active, pressures d5 and e6, good control over diagonals.\n  - d3-b5: Further on the same diagonal, attacks a6 and potentially threatens something if white has weaknesses, but may be overextended.\n  - d3-a6: Too far, extremely passive, loses tempo.\n\n- King moves:\n  - e4-d4/d5/e5/f4/f5: All aim to centralize or advance. d5, e5, f5 push forward, possibly chasing something or aiming for opposition.\n  - e4-e3/f3: Retreat slightly, less active.\n\nMost promising moves:\n- B: d3-c4 — gains strong central influence, eyes b5, a6, d5, e6, and keeps flexibility.\n- K: e4-e5 — advances the king, fights for space, potentially cuts off enemy king or supports future operations.\n- B: d3-e2 — safe, supports king, prepares coordination.\n\nHowever, with minimal material, king activity often wins such endgames. Pushing the king forward increases its power. e4-e5 is a strong candidate as it seizes space and limits any potential white king movement (though white king is on a2, possibly stuck).\n\nBut wait — is there a risk? White has a pawn on a7 (PWF1), which already moved and cannot move further unless it promotes. But it's on a7, rank 7, so it's one step from promotion. However, it's white's pawn, not black's. So a7 is occupied by a *white* pawn. That means if it's White's turn next, they could play a7-a8=Q. But it's currently Black's turn.\n\nWe must prevent that promotion! If we don't stop it, White will queen and win easily.\n\nThe pawn is on a7, moving toward a8. It's already marked PWF1 — meaning it has moved before, and did NOT move two squares last turn (so en passant not possible). But it's on a7 — so it can move to a8 next turn and promote.\n\nWe must capture it or blockade it — but our bishop is on d3, which can go to c4, b5, a6 — yes! a6 is reachable via d3-a6.\n\nWait: d3-a6 is a valid move? Yes, along the diagonal. From d3 to a6: d3 → c4 → b5 → a6. Same color? d3 is a dark square (d-file even, 3 odd → even+odd=odd → dark), a6: a-file odd, 6 even → odd+even=odd → dark. Yes, same color.\n\nSo B: d3-a6 puts the bishop on a6, directly attacking the pawn on a7. Then if White tries a7-a8=Q, we can capture the new queen: Bxa8.\n\nThat’s critical!\n\nAlternatively, B: d3-b5 also eyes a7, but from b5, we don’t directly attack a7 — because a7 is on the same diagonal? b5 to a7: b5→a6→a7 — no, that’s not diagonal. Diagonally, b5 attacks a6 and c7, not a7. a7 is one file left and two ranks up from b5 — not diagonal. So only from c4 or a6 can we attack a7?\n\nLet’s compute:\n\n- Bishop on d3:\n  - a6: d3→c4→b5→a6 — yes, diagonal, same color.\n  - Does a6 attack a7? No — a6 and a7 are on same file, not diagonal.\n  - To attack a7, bishop must be on a diagonal with a7.\n\na7 is on the a7-h0 diagonal? What squares are on the diagonal with a7?\n\nFrom a7: goes to b6, c5, d4, e3, f2, g1.\n\nSo only those squares attack a7.\n\nOur bishop on d3 — d3 is d3, not d4. d3 is one rank below d4. So d3 does NOT attack a7.\n\ne3 would be on that diagonal? e3 → d4 → c5 → b6 → a7 — yes! e3 is on the diagonal a7-g1.\n\nBut our bishop is on d3 — which is not on that diagonal.\n\nCan our king reach e3? Yes — K: e4-e3 is a valid move.\n\nIf we play K: e4-e3, then the king is on e3, which is on the diagonal of a7. Then, after White plays a7-a8=Q, we can play Kxq — Ke3xa7? Wait — a7 is not adjacent to e3.\n\nDistance: e3 to a7 is 4 files left, 4 ranks up — knight move? No, king moves one square. So even if on e3, king cannot capture on a7.\n\nOnly a piece on b6, c5, d4, e3, f2, or g1 can attack a7.\n\nCurrently, none of our pieces are on those squares.\n\nOur bishop on d3 — can it get to d4? d3 to d4 is vertical — bishops move diagonally, not vertically. So no.\n\nCan the bishop ever reach d4? Only if it were on a diagonal to d4 — like a1, b2, c3, e5, f6, g7, h8 — but d3 is not aligned with d4.\n\nSo bishop cannot reach d4.\n\nClosest attacking square the bishop can reach is... let's see:\n\nTo attack a7, must be on b6, c5, d4, e3, f2, g1.\n\nCan bishop go to c5? From d3 to c5: d3→c4→b5→a6 — not going to c5. d3 to c5 is one left, two up — not diagonal. Diagonally, d3 to c4 is one option, or e4, etc. d3 to c2, b1, e2, f1 — down; or c4, b5, a6 — up-left.\n\nNo path to c5, b6, d4, etc.\n\nSo bishop cannot attack a7 at all.\n\nKing cannot attack a7 either — too far.\n\nSo we cannot stop the pawn from promoting.\n\nBut wait — is it White's turn after us? No — it's our turn now. We move first.\n\nCurrent state: White has a pawn on a7. It hasn't moved yet this turn. It's Black's turn.\n\nAfter we move, White will move. White can play a7-a8=Q and promote.\n\nWe cannot prevent that.\n\nBut why hasn't White already promoted? Because it's not their turn — we are to move now.\n\nSo after our move, White will promote and have a queen.\n\nWe must prepare for that.\n\nWith a queen, White can deliver checkmate quickly unless we have a way to survive.\n\nBut we have a bishop and king. Can we avoid mate?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the pawn on a7 is *our* pawn? No — it's labeled PWF1 — Pawn, White, 2-step=False, Moved=1. So it's White's pawn.\n\nYes.\n\nSo inevitable: next White will promote to queen.\n\nTherefore, we must use this move to improve our king or bishop to survive the coming storm.\n\nBest plan: centralize the king, prepare to face checks.\n\nOur king is on e4. Best squares: e5, f5, d5 — more central.\n\nK: e4-e5 is excellent — central, hard to checkmate, ready to oppose.\n\nBishop should stay active.\n\nBut bishop on d3 — best move might be to e2 or c4 to avoid being trapped.\n\nBut after promotion, White will have Q on a8.\n\nFrom a8, queen can go anywhere.\n\nOur king should not stay on open files.\n\ne5 is better than e4? Slightly more central.\n\nf4, f5 — maybe safer?\n\nBut no shelter.\n\nAlternatively, king to f3 — closer to center?\n\nBut e5 controls more space.\n\nIn king + bishop vs queen endgames, the side with the queen usually wins, but drawing chances exist if the king is near the center and active.\n\nOur best bet is to centralize the king and keep the bishop active.\n\nTop move: K: e4-e5 — improves king position, fights for opposition, prepares to zigzag against checks.\n\nBishop move won't help much — it